What is Robustness? The Extinction Cascade















Co-extinction simulation (Memmott et al. 2004) on Handrkov pollination web




 🟢 Peripheral Spoke Loss   \(R \approx 0.85\) 



✈️ &lt;strong&gt;Aviation:&lt;/strong&gt; Regional airfields fail (&lt;em&gt;Røros&lt;/em&gt;) ➔ &lt;strong&gt;Continental grid intact&lt;/strong&gt;


🌸 &lt;strong&gt;Meadow:&lt;/strong&gt; Peripheral flora lost (&lt;em&gt;Lathyrus&lt;/em&gt;) ➔ &lt;strong&gt;Pollinators buffered by core&lt;/strong&gt;



✓ High Structural Redundancy • Shock Absorbed




 🔴 Core Hub Loss   \(R \approx 0.45\) 



✈️ &lt;strong&gt;Aviation:&lt;/strong&gt; Mega-hubs shut (&lt;em&gt;Heathrow&lt;/em&gt;) ➔ &lt;strong&gt;Transit paralyzed&lt;/strong&gt;


🌸 &lt;strong&gt;Meadow:&lt;/strong&gt; 6 Core Hubs wither (&lt;em&gt;Centaurea&lt;/em&gt;) ➔ &lt;strong&gt;Secondary cascades&lt;/strong&gt;



⚠ Systemic Vulnerability • The Achilles’ Heel




The 15-Year Question: In Handrkov, did extreme climate warming break our core hubs—or did the core hold?


Slide 6: Here is how network theory formalizes robustness (Memmott et al. 2004). Robustness R is the area under the co-extinction curve: as we remove plant species, how many pollinators survive? Click 1: When we remove peripheral species first (red dashed curve), the curve stays flat near 100%! Almost all pollinators survive because they have the core. Just like closing a regional airfield in Norway leaves European flights running. Robustness R is high (0.85). Click 2: BUT when we remove the core hubs first (solid navy curve), the curve drops off a cliff! Losing Centaurea and Succisa deprives pollinators of their main food, causing secondary extinction cascades. Robustness plunges to 0.45! Click 3: This sets up our central ecological dilemma: Across 15 years, during unprecedented summer droughts, did climate stress break our core hubs, or did the core species buffer the community? Let’s look at the climate history of our meadow!
